Using iperf for traffic:
- Since i am using a VM. the VM is my server and my host machine which is windows is my client.
- that means windows is attacking my VM.
- i have done this, as windows is an exeternal machine, it will hit the NIC of the VM and thus our xdp prog will work. 
- install iperf3 in both VM and windows(host machine)
- in VM open new terminal and execute this => iperf3 -s
- then in windows shift + right click on the iperf download folder and choose run from powershell.
- then execute this command in powershell => .\iperf3.exe -c 192.168.1.66 -u -b 50M -t 20
- NOTE : replace this 192.168.1.66 with your vm Ip address. you can do that by executing: ip addr show ens33
- NOTE : ens33 is the network interface which is different for different VMS to check what yours is execute: ip link.



FLOW OF THE PROJECT: (this is exactly how to run the project)
1. sudo ./scripts/load_xdp.sh
2. new terminal, in vm => iperf3 -s
3. in windows (or your host machine) => .\iperf3.exe -c 192.168.1.66 -u -b 50M -t 20
4. in vm, new terminal, activate venv, => sudo .venv/bin/python userspace/stats_reader.py
5. observe the traffic. 
6. UNLOAD THE XDP => sudo ./scripts/unload_xdp.sh





